Here's a C program that meets your requirements. It prompts the user for a filename, reads the file, and outputs the line and position of every character in the file. The program also handles errors and ensures the file is closed properly.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    char filename[FILENAME_MAX];
    FILE *file;
    int line_number = 1;
    int char_position = 0;
    int c;

    printf("Enter the filename of a text file: ");
    scanf("%s", filename);

    file = fopen(filename, "r");

    if (file == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: Unable to open file %s.\n", filename);
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }

    while ((c = fgetc(file)) != EOF) {
        if (c == '\n') {
            line_number++;
            char_position = 0;
        } else {
            char_position++;
        }

        printf("Line %d, Position %d: Character '%c' (ASCII %d)\n", line_number, char_position, c, c);
    }

    fclose(file);

    return 0;
}
```

This program reads one character at a time using `fgetc()` and checks if the character is a newline (`\n`) to increment the line number and reset the character position. If the character is not a newline, it increments the character position. The program then prints the line number, character position, character, and its ASCII value for each character in the file.

