Here is a simple C program that does what you asked for. This program uses the `stdio.h` library for file handling and the `stdlib.h` library for string manipulation.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

void readFileAndPrintPositions(const char *filename) {
    FILE *file = fopen(filename, "r");
    if (file == NULL) {
        perror("Error opening file");
        return;
    }

    int line = 1;
    int position = 0;
    char c;

    while ((c = fgetc(file)) != EOF) {
        printf("Line %d, Position %d: %c\n", line, position++, c);

        if (c == '\n') {
            line++;
            position = 0;
        }
    }

    fclose(file);
}

int main() {
    char filename[256];

    printf("Enter the filename: ");
    scanf("%255s", filename);

    readFileAndPrintPositions(filename);

    return 0;
}
```

This program reads a file line by line and prints the line number, position within the line, and the character. It uses `fgetc` to read each character one by one and increments the position counter for each character read. When it encounters a newline character, it increments the line number and resets the position counter.

Please note that this program assumes that the file is not too large to fit into memory. If the file is very large, you may need to use a different approach, such as reading the file in chunks or using a stream-based approach.

Also, this program does not handle errors such as invalid filenames or non-existent files. You may want to add error handling code to make it more robust.

Lastly, remember to compile this program with a C compiler, such as gcc:

```sh
gcc filename.c -o filename
```

Then you can run it with:

```sh
./filename
```

And enter the filename when prompted.

