 In ASP.NET, you can read content from an HTTP request using the `HttpRequest` object. This object provides various properties and methods to access different parts of the request, such as query strings, form data, headers, and the request body.

Here's a basic example of how to read content from an HTTP request in an ASP.NET Web API controller:

### Reading Query String Parameters
```csharp
public IHttpActionResult Get()
{
    var queryString = HttpContext.Current.Request.QueryString;
    var name = queryString["name"];
    var age = queryString["age"];

    return Ok(new { Name = name, Age = age });
}
```

### Reading Form Data
```csharp
public IHttpActionResult Post()
{
    var form = HttpContext.Current.Request.Form;
    var name = form["name"];
    var age = form["age"];

    return Ok(new { Name = name, Age = age });
}
```

### Reading Request Body (JSON)
To read the request body, especially when dealing with JSON data, you can use the `Request.Content` property in an ASP.NET Web API controller.

```csharp
public async Task<IHttpActionResult> Post()
{
    var content = await Request.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
    var data = JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<dynamic>(content);

    var name = data.name;
    var age = data.age;

    return Ok(new { Name = name, Age = age });
}
```

### Reading Headers
```csharp
public IHttpActionResult Get()
{
    var headers = HttpContext.Current.Request.Headers;
    var userAgent = headers["User-Agent"];

    return Ok(new { UserAgent = userAgent });
}
```

### Complete Example in ASP.NET Web API Controller
Here's a complete example of an ASP.NET Web API controller that reads different parts of the request:

```csharp
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using System.Web.Http;
using Newtonsoft.Json;

public class MyController : ApiController
{
    // GET api/my
    public IHttpActionResult Get()
    {
        var queryString = HttpContext.Current.Request.QueryString;
        var name = queryString["name"];
        var age = queryString["age"];

        var headers = HttpContext.Current.Request.Headers;
        var userAgent = headers["User-Agent"];

        return Ok(new { Name = name, Age = age, UserAgent = userAgent });
    }

    // POST api/my
    public async Task<IHttpActionResult> Post()
    {
        var form = HttpContext.Current.Request.Form;
        var name = form["name"];
        var age = form["age"];

        var content = await Request.Content.ReadAsStringAsync();
        var data = JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<dynamic>(content);

        var jsonName = data.name;
        var jsonAge = data.age;

        return Ok(new { FormName = name, FormAge = age, JsonName = jsonName, JsonAge = jsonAge });
    }
}
```

### Notes
- Make sure to include the necessary namespaces, such as `System.Web`, `System.Net.Http`, and `Newtonsoft.Json`.
- For reading JSON content, you might need to install the `Newtonsoft.Json` package via NuGet if it's not already included in your project.

This should give you a good starting point for reading various parts of an HTTP request in an ASP.NET application.

