In ASP.NET, you can read content from a request using the `HttpRequest` object. Here's how you can do it in both ASP.NET Web Forms and ASP.NET Core:

**ASP.NET Web Forms (C#):**

```csharp
public void YourMethod(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    using (StreamReader reader = new StreamReader(Request.InputStream, Encoding.UTF8))
    {
        string content = reader.ReadToEndAsync().Result;
        // Now you can use the 'content' variable which contains the request content
    }
}
```

**ASP.NET Core (C#):**

```csharp
[HttpPost]
public async Task<IActionResult> YourMethod()
{
    using (StreamReader reader = new StreamReader(Request.Body, Encoding.UTF8))
    {
        string content = await reader.ReadToEndAsync();
        // Now you can use the 'content' variable which contains the request content
    }

    // Rest of your code
}
```

In both examples, replace `YourMethod` with the name of your method, and make sure to handle any exceptions that might occur during the reading process.

If you're working with JSON data, you might want to deserialize it into a strongly-typed object. Here's an example using `System.Text.Json` in ASP.NET Core:

```csharp
[HttpPost]
public async Task<IActionResult> YourMethod()
{
    using (StreamReader reader = new StreamReader(Request.Body, Encoding.UTF8))
    {
        string content = await reader.ReadToEndAsync();
        YourModel model = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<YourModel>(content);
        // Now you can use the 'model' object which contains the deserialized JSON data
    }

    // Rest of your code
}
```
